  <nav class="qa-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Compare · Identification</p>
    <h1>Raven vs Crow Size: How Much Bigger Is a Raven?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> A Common Raven is approximately 25–35% longer and 2–3× heavier than a Carrion Crow or American Crow. In flight, a raven appears roughly Buzzard-sized — a substantial, broad-winged bird with a wedge-shaped tail — where a crow reads as pigeon-sized with a flat or rounded tail. Size is a reliable field mark in direct comparison but needs supporting field marks when birds are seen alone.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Measurements at a Glance</h2>
    <div class="compare-table-wrap">
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Common Raven</th><th>Carrion Crow</th><th>American Crow</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Length (cm)</td><td>60–68</td><td>44–51</td><td>40–53</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weight (g)</td><td>800–1,600</td><td>370–650</td><td>316–620</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wingspan (cm)</td><td>115–150</td><td>84–100</td><td>85–100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bill length (mm)</td><td>55–70</td><td>35–45</td><td>30–40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tail shape</td><td>Wedge / diamond</td><td>Rounded / squarish</td><td>Rounded</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Source: del Hoyo et al. (2009).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Using Size as a Field Mark</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Size identification works best in certain conditions and fails in others:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Direct comparison:</strong> raven and crow together in the same field of view — size difference is obvious and diagnostic. Experienced birders often see ravens arrive at a carcass and displace crows, making the size relationship immediately visible.</li>
      <li><strong>Flight silhouette:</strong> in flight, the raven's size relative to other birds (pigeons, buzzards, gulls) is usually usable. A raven at any distance shows the breadth and length of a large raptor; a crow reads small by comparison.</li>
      <li><strong>Isolated individual:</strong> without a reference, a single bird at an unfamiliar distance can be misjudged. A large crow flying over open ground with no reference can look raven-sized; a raven in a large landscape can look crow-sized.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Standard birding practice is to use size together with tail shape, bill profile, and vocalisation for a confident identification.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why Are Ravens So Much Larger?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The size difference between ravens and crows reflects their different ecological niches:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens specialise in large-animal carrion — opening carcasses of ungulates (moose, caribou, deer) requires a powerful bill and body mass. A crow is not strong enough to open a frozen deer carcass; a raven can access it.</li>
      <li>Ravens occupy more open, elevated habitats — mountains, arctic tundra, sea cliffs — where larger body size provides thermoregulation advantages in cold conditions (Bergmann's Rule).</li>
      <li>Ravens require larger territories and longer-range flights than crows — their body size supports the sustained soaring and long-distance foraging that crow physiology cannot.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs crow (full comparison)</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/common-raven-vs-american-crow/">Common raven vs American crow</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-big-are-ravens/">How big are ravens?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-do-you-tell-a-raven-from-a-crow-in-flight/">How do you tell a raven from a crow in flight?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="qa-faq-item">
        <summary class="qa-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="qa-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="qa-sources qa-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="qa-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">del Hoyo, J., Elliott, A. & Christie, D. (eds.) (2009). <em>Handbook of the Birds of the World</em>, Vol. 14. Lynx Edicions. — all measurements, tail shape, ecological context.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Boarman, W.I. & Heinrich, B. (1999). Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). In Poole, A. (ed.), <em>The Birds of North America</em>, No. 476. Cornell Lab of Ornithology. — size range, bill dimensions, flight silhouette identification.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="qa-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> peer-reviewed data</p>
  </section>

</article>
</BaseLayout>

